#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527240} :activity/id #uuid "0ef48a11-b676-522a-82bb-9f39665ac76e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050027 :topic/id #uuid "26b9f4f0-7964-52b3-9a23-d31ae6927cb5" :topic/math-academy-id 1939} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527235 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403468 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74e84be6-fb21-5888-bde0-2b1fc8861800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨-3,-4,1⟩+t\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403470} {:answer/id #uuid "125b86ba-8a17-5cc7-bb9b-688c88040c9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨3,-4,1⟩+t\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403471} {:answer/id #uuid "a563a551-487d-52b0-b121-65d93d53f35f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,⟨3,4,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403472} {:answer/id #uuid "74e61dbd-b0b0-51fb-aee1-a797c08c8fb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,⟨3,-4,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403473} {:answer/id #uuid "65d0bd41-c0f7-5813-a9d5-53b92a3b0d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,⟨-3,-4,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403474}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403474} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a86f3ea-971f-5c0c-ab0d-7a86359ca0e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ca7dad4d-8007-5c21-9019-38d66b5366c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-71407" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface $z=2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x \\\\ z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x)k \\\\  & =(-4x-1)\\text{ }i-4y\\text{ }j+k \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3) & =(-4(\\frac{1}{2})-1)\\,i-4(1)\\text{ }j+k \\\\  & =-3\\,i-4\\,j+k \\\\  & =⟨-3,-4,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)$ and passes through the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,⟨-3,-4,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"} :step/id #uuid "17075a61-c9d0-5adb-a8b2-23c29d75a027" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527258}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527240 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125074 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccafabf2-27cf-5b44-8930-a54e452bc954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-y-1=\\frac{z-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385385} {:answer/id #uuid "67a0969d-2efa-50ec-a3a4-619fb8bb16fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{y+1}{2}=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385386} {:answer/id #uuid "4e6a97a3-5af6-531a-870a-ae08853e320f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{2}=z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385387} {:answer/id #uuid "7632f3d8-1e29-5bdf-93fe-9967dfc64633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{y+1}{2}=\\frac{z-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385388} {:answer/id #uuid "247f5172-7db7-511c-8fd9-7db9f6287753" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=y+1=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385389}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385386} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b6b56a1-3889-545f-a00d-30961c2c2ad7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "78adb807-d26f-59bb-a8f7-9d0e5cc88b53" :question/math-academy-id "q-37515" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2}$ at the point $(0,-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2} \\\\ 0 & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2}-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2}-z$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,-1,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,-1,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2}-z)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2}-z)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2}-z)k \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i+(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+2y)\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(0,-1,1) & =(-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\text{ }i+[(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+2(-1)]\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\  & =-i-2\\,j-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-0}{-1}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{-2}=\\frac{z-1}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x}{-1}=\\frac{y+1}{-2}=\\frac{z-1}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x}{1}=\\frac{y+1}{2}=\\frac{z-1}{1} \\\\ x=\\frac{y+1}{2}=z-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2598a4b3-3e2b-55cf-8762-35fa24bff20e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527256}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527256 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125078 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "284eed2b-f5b5-55e8-8043-dc7f9352df8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\,i-2\\,j+4\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385391} {:answer/id #uuid "891daeba-0e3b-599d-ba1c-f3ca19efea95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4\\,i-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385392} {:answer/id #uuid "7479fa2d-d3ba-5e5f-b91f-c455aeddd593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\,i+j+4\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385393} {:answer/id #uuid "f368beea-18d3-5ba4-a48e-57ffd46d193b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i+2\\,j+4\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385394} {:answer/id #uuid "4cb18261-78e6-5c3a-b541-e09f14d04051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385395}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385393} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6793d18-67c7-5552-bd0c-a7ad3c9a1562" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5878a311-1e95-57bd-8bd7-391f384f5bbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-37570" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=xy+5x+{z}^{2}$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,2)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-1,2).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+5x+{z}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+5x+{z}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy+5x+{z}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =(y+5)\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j+2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,2) & =(-1+5)\\,i+(1)\\,j+2(2)\\,k \\\\  & =4\\,i+j+4\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $4\\,i+j+4\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,2).$"} :step/id #uuid "628d3218-9eb5-5ad9-a24c-b47a38223247" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527262}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527258 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527143 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a0e0626-6248-5b0f-8302-6f527732a13c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-2y+3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526559} {:answer/id #uuid "253ca439-cd38-5c0a-a233-00c9ea58b381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x+y+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526584} {:answer/id #uuid "48db2746-13e6-5da9-a526-f85be7161a7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-y+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526647} {:answer/id #uuid "6545d507-c4ab-5302-a22d-62c4ef0a3e20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526705} {:answer/id #uuid "fdd0a5cd-65c6-58a5-ac60-ab333212dddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527006}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526584} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdf514b2-8d3a-5a58-a6b9-5e62c2d37fa5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "28dded9d-d02a-5f94-8a43-d32ef32c8465" :question/math-academy-id "q-130720" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${x}^{3}{y}^{2}=2x+y.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}{y}^{2} & =2x+y \\\\ {x}^{3}{y}^{2}-2x-y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{3}{y}^{2}-2x-y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}{y}^{2}-2x-y)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}{y}^{2}-2x-y)j \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-2)\\,i+(2{x}^{3}y-1)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-1,1) & =(3(-1)^{2}(1)^{2}-2)\\,i+(2(-1)^{3}(1)-1)\\,j \\\\  & =\\,i-3\\,j \\\\  & =⟨1,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(-1,1)=⟨1,-3⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,-3).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(-1)}{1} & =\\frac{y-1}{(-3)} \\\\ -3(x+1) & =y-1 \\\\ -3x-3 & =y-1 \\\\ 3x+y+2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6839775f-3cbd-5361-8538-0f08a1251e91"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527262 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527163 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fc28fd4-0332-5dca-9124-fbc2512f4f90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526573} {:answer/id #uuid "47b2da7a-a030-51c5-b002-05391f108200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i+j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526643} {:answer/id #uuid "c8f30ca4-75c7-5d71-83ef-390f43fece84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}i+j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526890} {:answer/id #uuid "e2579daa-2b83-50d8-a9be-1387a386e9ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526945} {:answer/id #uuid "e8e5fc00-b4cc-5045-8cb1-ac186e6219f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526964}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526643} :answer-field/id #uuid "f14cf0d4-83ef-5fb3-b242-66382b80f326" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4bf356ec-77aa-55f5-9268-fd51c57a8d9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-71400" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,1,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,1,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz))\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz))\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{z}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,1,1) & =\\frac{1}{1}\\,i+\\frac{1}{1}\\,j+\\frac{1}{1}\\,k \\\\  & =i+j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $i+j+k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,1,1).$"} :step/id #uuid "7d9f5645-ab76-539c-abb6-92fcd4f5fb7f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527235}}] :activity/title "The Gradient as a Normal Vector" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526486}]}
